Java/SpringBoot/Spring-Annotations.content:
Annotations
2025-10-06
tags: - 🌱 indexSpring

Stereotype Annotations: Fundamental annotations that mark a class so that Spring recognizes it and manages its lifecycle (Beans).
Spring Boot “Core” Annotations: Specific Spring Boot annotations used to drastically simplify configuration and scheduling.
Dependency Injection Annotations: Used to “wire” beans to each other and to configure the Spring container (injecting objects or values).
WEB MVC Injection Annotations: Essential for building the web layer and REST APIs (mapping URLs and extracting data).
Aspects and Transactions Annotations: Used to handle cross-cutting concerns.

Stereotype Annotations
These are the most fundamental annotations. They mark a class so that Spring recognizes it and manages its lifecycle (in other words, transforms it into a “Bean”).
@Component
@Component: Tells Spring: “Hey, manage this class!“.
What is a Component (or Bean)?
Normally, in Java, you create objects yourself using the new keyword (e.g., new MyClass()). When you mark a class with @Component, you are handing over that control to Spring (this is called Inversion of Control - IoC).
A Component is simply a Java class that Spring will automatically instantiate, store in its memory (the Application Context), manage its lifecycle, and inject into other parts of your code whenever needed. When a class becomes a Component managed by Spring, we call it a Spring Bean.
@Service
@Service: A specialization of @Component. Used to mark classes in the service layer (where business rules reside).
@Repository
@Repository: A specialization used for classes in the data access layer (DAO - Data Access Object). It also enables the translation of specific database exceptions into Spring exceptions.
@Controller
@Controller: A specialization used for classes that handle web requests in the traditional MVC architecture, usually returning the name of a view (like an HTML page).
@RestController
@RestController: A combination of @Controller and @ResponseBody. Used to create REST APIs, where methods return data (like JSON) directly in the response body.
“
Spring Boot “Core” Annotations
Spring Boot adds its own annotations to drastically simplify configuration.
@SpringBootApplication
@SpringBootApplication is the heart of any Spring Boot application, combining component scanning, auto-configuration, and Java-based configuration into a single annotation.
@EnableScheduling

@EnableScheduling: Enables the execution of @Scheduled methods periodically. This annotation is placed in your app.java.

@Scheduled

@Scheduled: Indicates that the annotated method will occur periodically according to a defined time.


SPRING BOOT DOC: &gt; Annotation that marks a method to be scheduled. For periodic tasks, exactly one of the cron(), fixedDelay(), or fixedRate() attributes must be specified, and additionally an optional initialDelay(). For a one-time task, it is sufficient to just specify an initialDelay().

Dependency Injection Annotations
These annotations are used to “wire” beans to each other and to configure the Spring container.
@Autowired
@Autowired: Asks Spring to inject an instance of a bean into a field, constructor, or method - Automatically wired.
It is important to emphasize that this decorator is not needed when a class has just one constructor. Actually, is an anti-pattern to use this when you do not have more than one constructor because it hides it’s proprieties (spring &gt;  4.3).

Pt-br: “Evito usar @Autowired em campo porque esconde as dependências e dificulta testes unitários. Prefiro injeção por construtor, e desde o Spring 4.3 não preciso nem da anotação se a classe tiver um único construtor.”

@Value

While @Autowired injects objects (other beans), @Value injects simple values (Strings, numbers, booleans, etc.).

// Injects the value of the &#039;app.static.location&#039; property from application.properties
@Value(&lt;x&gt;) // &lt;x&gt; = Path that delivers the value, ex: &quot;${app.static.location}&quot;
private String staticLocation;
WEB MVC Injection Annotations
Essential for building the web layer and REST APIs.
@RequestMapping
@RequestMapping: The main annotation for mapping request URLs to specific methods in a @Controller or @RestController.
@&lt;x&gt;Mappings
@GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping: Shortcuts for @RequestMapping for the respective HTTP verbs.
@PathVariable
@PathVariable: Extracts a value from the URL (e.g., the id in /users/{id}).
@RequestParam
@RequestParam: Extracts a parameter from the URL query string (e.g., ?name=kkphoenix).
@RequestBody
@RequestBody: Converts the body of an HTTP request (usually JSON) into a Java object.
Persistence/ORM Annotations
Persistence Annotations
- JPA (Jakarta Persistence API)
@Entity
Defines a class as a entity in persistence
@Entity
public class SomeClass { }
@Id
Defines a value as the ID of a entity
@Entity
public class SomeClass {
	@Id
	public Long id;
}
@GeneratedValue
Defines that the value of a variable will be auto generated in persistence.
@GeneratedValue(strategy = SEQUENCE, generator = &quot;CUST_SEQ&quot;)
The GeneratedValue annotation may be applied to a primary key property or field of an entity or mapped superclass in conjunction with the Id annotation. The persistence provider is only required to support the GeneratedValue for simple primary keys. Use of the GeneratedValue annotation for derived primary keys is not supported. ~SPRING vscode docs
@Entity
public class SomeClass {
	@Id
	@GeneratedValue( strategy = GenerationType.AUTO )
	public Long id;
}
@JoinTable
It says that from a attribute, will be created a join table.

name =  defines the name of the join table
joinColumns = defines the first part of the join
inverseJoinColumns = defines the other part of the join

@Entity
public class SomeClass {

	@JoinTable(
		name = &quot;someclass_otherCLass&quot;,
		joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = &quot;someclass_id&quot;),
		inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = &quot;otherclass_id&quot;)
	)
	private Set&lt;OtherClass&gt; others;
}
Relations

@ManyToMany = @ManyToMany(mappedBy, targetEntity, cascade, fetch ) &lt;Array&gt;&lt;type&gt; &lt;attribute&gt; = &lt;value&gt; = This implies that for each object of this type has many objects of &lt;type&gt;  inside &lt;Array&gt;, and for each object of &lt;type&gt; inside array has many objects of this type. This creates a third table called &lt;this&gt;_&lt;type&gt; that will represent those relations.

mappedBy = who maps the relation (who saves data inside the join table), if not described, it will create two tables, one for each object as the one who maps the relation.
cascade = defines if &lt;type&gt; objects are going to be deleted too on delete of &lt;this&gt; object. It has a CascadeType enum with ALL, DETACH, MERGE, PERSIST, REFRESH, REMOVE.

ALL: Applies all cascade operations (save, update, delete, etc.) simultaneously.
PERSIST: Automatically saves child entities to the database when the parent object is saved.
MERGE: Updates the state of child entities in the database when the parent object is updated.
REMOVE: Automatically deletes child entities from the database when the parent object is deleted.
REFRESH: Synchronizes child data with the database when reloading the parent object’s information.
DETACH: Disconnects child objects from JPA/Hibernate management when the parent is detached.


targetEntity = what is the target class, the &lt;type&gt;. If you are using generics, things goes automatically, but if you need for a reason, it exists.
fetch = defines if when you load &lt;this&gt; persistence object, you load &lt;type&gt; object too.  EAGER = it will load automatically, LAZY = just when you ask for it. default = LAZY.



Equality
there is two methods that are used to determinate how hibernate is going to determinate equality Indo mais fundo ( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°), equals() and hashCode(), if there is a implementation of those, hibernate will use it.
Aspects and Transactions Annotations
To handle cross-cutting concerns.

Java/SpringBoot/Spring-Interfaces.content:
Spring Interfaces
2026-02-07
tags: - 🌱 indexSpring
Spring is built on the principle of interface-oriented programming. This means you couple code to contracts (interfaces), and Spring takes care of providing the correct implementation at runtime.
These categories are not native to Spring, but they are a very good way to study the content and group the knowledge.

Strategy Interfaces
Callback Interfaces (Lifecycle &amp; Aware)
Configuration Interfaces
Template Interfaces (Template Method Patterns)

Strategy Interfaces
This is the most important and fundamental category. These are interfaces that define a contract for an operation, allowing different implementations (strategies) to be swapped without changing the code that uses them. This is the direct application of the Strategy design pattern.
CrudRepository
The CrudRepository is a perfect example of a Strategy Interface in Spring Data. It defines a standard contract for CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete) on an entity, and Spring provides the actual implementation at runtime (usually interacting with JPA/Hibernate).
import org.springframework.data.repository.CrudRepository;
import com.suaempresa.projeto.model.Usuario;

public interface UsuarioRepository extends CrudRepository&lt;Usuario, Long&gt; {
    // You can also define your own strategy by naming methods properly:
    Usuario findByEmail(String email);
}
Lifecycle &amp; Aware
These are interfaces that allow your beans to “react” to events in the Spring container lifecycle or become “aware” (Aware) of their environment. Spring “calls back” methods defined in these interfaces at specific moments.
CommandLineRunner
The CommandLineRunner interface is a Lifecycle interface that will execute exactly between when app is ready and app is receiving requests and sending responses. It will execute defined run method when app is ready, it is pretty commonly used when seeding databases and things like that, usually created inside bootstrap.
import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class DatabaseSeeder implements CommandLineRunner {

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) throws Exception {
        // This code executes automatically exactly once during application startup!
        System.out.println(&quot;Application has started! Seeding the database...&quot;);
    }
}
Configuration Interfaces
These are interfaces that you implement to customize the behavior of the Spring Framework. Instead of Spring calling your code to react to an event, it is your code that informs Spring how it should behave.
Template Interfaces
Although they are often abstract classes and not pure interfaces, they follow the same principle. They provide a “template” for an operation that handles all the repetitive and low-level logic (like opening/closing connections, handling exceptions), letting you focus only on the important part of the work.

